            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed around the frame of your back door, at electrical outlets, and at the interior wall between your living room and the sun room area.



I did not note any air leakage through your two bathroom fans, but as they are both quite noisy I recommend replacing them with ENERGY STAR® rated models.



While I did note some locations where air sealing could be done on it's own, other upgrades I have recommended will also affect your air leakage rate.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $260&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with R20 fibreglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



Adding insulation to the exterior of your walls is a significant renovation, and we do not typically recommend it unless your siding is at the end of it's life. You have wood siding, and during my evaluation I noted that it appears to be in excellent shape. If this is an upgrade you are planning, you could save the wood siding and reinstall it after adding insulation. This will reduce your material costs but increase your labour costs. If you decide to do this, you will need to build out the corners to account for the additional thickness of the walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $700&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;23.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x4 framing with R12 fibreglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, which could be done at the same time as your exterior walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $335&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</foundation>
                <windows>

Some of your windows have already been replaced, while some have not. In general, your windows are in good shape, but I did notice some condensation on your upstairs windows which could indicate that the seals are compomised. I have modelled replacing all of your older windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



This upgrade would best be done at the same time as a siding and insulation retrofit, but can be done at any time.



While I have not modelled upgrading your doors in this report, should you need to replace any of them, look for ENERGY STAR® qualified doors rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of your older windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $265&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your domestic hot water (DHW) is currenly provided by your oil fired boiler. A more efficient boiler will result in savings for space heating as well as DHW. Listed below are the savings associated with DHW for the different options I modelled under space heating. 



Option 1: Replace current 85.3% efficient oil boiler with an 87% efficient oil boiler.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

In addition to the space heating savings associated with this upgrade, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $21&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



Option 2: Replace current 85.3% efficient oil boiler with a 95% efficient propane fired boiler.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

In addition to the space heating savings associated with this upgrade, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85.3% AFUE. As you mentioned that you are considering replacing your boiler, I have modelled a couple of options for you. 



Your domestic hot water is currently provided off the boiler and stored in an indirect storage tank. I have used this same set up for all the upgrade options. 



Since you also indicated that your boiler has a hard time keeping up with the DHW demand during the winter, I would strongly recommend that you talk to a heating contractor to make sure that your boiler is sized correctly. 



The chimney in the corner of your mechanical room is providing combustion air to your boiler, and is bringing cold air into your home. Upgrading your boiler could allow for this chimney to be eliminated altogether, reducing air leakage. 



Option 1: I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil boiler with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, oil-fired boiler with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $473&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



Option 2: I have modelled replacing your oil fired boiler with an an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,437&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;38.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Finally, I have modelled the impact your pellet stove is having on your heating costs. I modelled using your pellet stove to provide 25% of your heating load on your main floor.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your pellet stove to meet 25% of your heating load on you main floor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $470&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and increasing your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to learn the systems usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

